Highlights:
1. A signal amplification strategy was based on target-catalyzed hairpin assembly and metal-organic frameworks (PdNPs@Fe-MOFs) mimetic enzyme co-reaction.
2. A new type of multifunctional iron based metal-organic frameworks possessed of excellent peroxidase-like activity and was designed and applied for sandwich-type biosensor for the first time.
3. It was demonstrated that "inserting" method was superior to traditional "backfilling" method for the fabrication of nucleic acid biosensor.
4. The biosensor was with a detection limit of 0.003 fM (S/N=3) and a linear range from 0.01 fM to 10 pM.
5. The biosensor was successfully used for detecting miR-122 in human serum, and provided a rapid and efficient method for detecting drug-induced liver injury at early stage.
